            <ArticleTitle>The impact of race on analgesia use among pediatric emergency department patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>618-21</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jpeds.2014.04.059</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0022-3476(14)00417-X</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>We studied the effect of race on analgesia use in potentially pain-related pediatric emergency department visits using the National Hospital Ambulatory Medical Care Survey (2005-2010). There were independent patient- and hospital-level racial disparities when it came to the type of analgesia used, suggesting black children are treated differently even within hospitals with high numbers of black patients.</AbstractText>
